<h2 id="why-switch">Why teams compare Freshdesk alternatives</h2>
<p>Freshdesk can be a good fit for email-first support. Looking at alternatives does not mean it lacks every advanced feature. It means the buying team should test whether the exact plan matches its operating model.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Different cost model:</strong> Some vendors price by agent, some add AI usage charges, and others tie plans to ticket or resolution volume. The base seat price is not the full cost.</li><li><strong>Different support channel:</strong> A shared inbox, messaging-first support, voice, social support and ecommerce order questions can require different workflows. Compare the specific channel set, not only the product name.</li><li><strong>CRM context:</strong> Teams that already manage contacts, deals and customer activity in a CRM may prefer support work to sit in that same system.</li><li><strong>Collaboration:</strong> Some teams want a lighter shared inbox where sales, success and support can work together. Others need formal ticket ownership, routing and service targets.</li><li><strong>Reporting and controls:</strong> Before switching, confirm the reports, permission controls, SLAs, audit options and routing rules that are actually included in the plan.</li><li><strong>Migration requirements:</strong> A tool may look better until you learn that a required field, attachment, comment or report cannot move in the way you expected.</li></ul>
<h2 id="zendesk">1. Zendesk: best for a broader support suite</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.zendesk.com/">Zendesk</a> is the first direct comparison to make when you want ticketing with structured ownership, customer context, automation and reporting. The vendor separates its Support plan from Suite plans, so confirm whether the features you need require the broader package.</p>
<p><strong>Price:</strong> Support Team is listed at US$19 per agent/month billed yearly; Suite Team is US$55 and Suite Professional is US$115. Checked October 28, 2026. See the <a href="https://www.zendesk.com/pricing/">official pricing page</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> a familiar ticket-based operating model, published plan distinctions and documented migration paths. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> total cost can rise when you need Suite features or additional AI and channel capabilities. Do not assume a feature listed on a higher tier is included in Support Team.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> your team only needs a simple shared inbox and would not use the additional controls. Read the <a href="/zendesk/review/">ToolboxKart Zendesk review</a> and the <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk comparison</a> before making the switch.</p>
<h2 id="zoho">2. Zoho Desk: best for small teams already using Zoho</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.zoho.com/desk/">Zoho Desk</a> is worth evaluating when your company already uses Zoho products or wants customer support connected to that wider environment. Its official pricing page lists a free edition for up to three users. Paid prices can vary by region, so check the price selector and the features for your country.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> a free starting point for a small team and a natural connection to the Zoho ecosystem. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> the right edition depends on the number of agents, reporting needs, automation and the other Zoho services you use.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you need a specific channel or workflow that is not confirmed in your selected edition. Before moving, check which ticket fields and conversation history can be exported and imported, then test a small data sample.</p>
<h2 id="helpscout">3. Help Scout: best for a shared inbox and help center</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.helpscout.com/">Help Scout</a> suits teams that organize support around shared conversations, saved replies and a searchable Docs site. Its official pricing page lists a Free plan for up to five users with one inbox and one Docs site. Standard is displayed at US$25 per user/month, Plus at US$45 and Pro at US$75. Checked October 28, 2026. Verify the billing toggle and current limits on the <a href="https://www.helpscout.com/pricing/">official pricing page</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> a clear shared-inbox model, customer-facing help content and a free entry point. Higher tiers add more workflows, SLA policies, routing and security options.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs:</strong> plan limits on inboxes, contacts, Docs sites and workflows matter as your team grows. The AI resolution add-on is priced separately at US$0.75 per resolution on the page checked, so include usage in the budget.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> your team needs highly customized ticket routing or enterprise controls that are not included in the chosen tier. It is most useful when collaboration and customer communication matter more than replicating every existing help desk configuration.</p>
<h2 id="front">4. Front: best for collaborative customer communication</h2>
<p><a href="https://front.com/">Front</a> is worth a look when support requests are mixed with account management, sales and other customer-facing work. It is organized around a shared workspace where teams can coordinate conversations and apply rules across their communication channels.</p>
<p><strong>Price:</strong> Front's official pricing page lists Starter from US$25 per seat/month. Checked October 28, 2026. Check the <a href="https://front.com/pricing">current plan page</a> for billing cadence, seat caps and channel restrictions before comparing it with Freshdesk.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> collaborative inbox workflows and a model that can suit teams where several departments work on the same customer relationship. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> channel access, workflow controls and analytics vary by plan. The Starter tier has limits that can make it unsuitable for a team that needs every channel or advanced routing.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> your support operation needs a conventional help desk with the exact ticket, portal and reporting behavior your current team relies on. Verify those workflows in a trial instead of assuming a shared inbox is a full replacement.</p>
<h2 id="hubspot">5. HubSpot Service Hub: best for CRM-led service</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/service">HubSpot Service Hub</a> is a strong candidate when support teams need to work from the same CRM that sales and customer success already use. The official page lists free service tools for up to two users. Paid plan details should be checked against your HubSpot edition and account setup.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> service activity can sit alongside contact and company records in the HubSpot CRM, which may reduce switching between systems for teams already using HubSpot. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> advanced service features and team capacity can depend on the paid tier. Some teams may need a broader HubSpot package to get the full workflow they expect.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you do not use HubSpot and mainly want a focused help desk. Compare the cost of the whole CRM setup, not only the service seat, and confirm the exact ticketing, automation and reporting features in the <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/pricing/service">official Service Hub pricing page</a>.</p>
<h2 id="intercom">6. Intercom: best for messaging-led support and AI</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.intercom.com/">Intercom</a> is a useful alternative when customer conversations, in-product messaging and AI resolution are central to the service model. Its official pricing page lists Essential from US$29 per seat/month billed annually and prices Fin AI Agent by resolution. Checked October 28, 2026. Review the <a href="https://www.intercom.com/pricing">official pricing page</a> for the current package and usage terms.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> messaging-first workflows and a pricing model that makes AI resolution a separate cost to model. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> outcome-based charges mean the monthly total depends on usage, and a messaging-centric system may not match every traditional ticketing process.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> your team does not need customer messaging or AI resolution and simply wants low-cost email ticketing. Calculate seats and expected AI resolutions separately before comparing the quote with Freshdesk.</p>
<h2 id="gorgias">7. Gorgias: best for ecommerce support</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.gorgias.com/">Gorgias</a> is designed for ecommerce support, where agents may need customer and order context while answering questions about shipping, returns and purchases. That focus can be useful for a store that wants its support workflow to sit close to commerce operations.</p>
<p><strong>Price:</strong> Gorgias bases pricing on monthly ticket volume. The public pricing view checked October 28, 2026 did not expose a stable starting amount in the text available, so this guide does not invent one. Check the <a href="https://www.gorgias.com/pricing">official pricing page</a> for your ticket volume and any AI usage charges.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> ecommerce-specific support workflows and a model that does not rely only on a per-agent price. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> ticket volume and AI usage can affect the budget, and the product focus may be less useful for a general service desk outside ecommerce.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you need broad internal service management or support workflows unrelated to commerce. Test order lookup, refund workflows, ticket history and export needs before moving a store's support operation.</p>
<h2 id="liveagent">8. LiveAgent: best for multi-channel help desk and live chat</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.liveagent.com/">LiveAgent</a> combines help desk functions with live chat and other customer contact channels. It is worth evaluating when the support team wants ticketing and live communication in one tool rather than stitching together several separate products.</p>
<p><strong>Price:</strong> the official page lists Starter at US$19/month or US$15/month with annual billing per agent. Checked October 28, 2026. Confirm the current offer, plan limits and <a href="https://www.liveagent.com/pricing">billing details</a> before purchase.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> a multi-channel approach and a published entry price. <strong>Trade-offs:</strong> the exact channel mix, integrations and reporting capabilities depend on the plan. Confirm that your support email, chat and other required channels are included before migrating.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> you need a particular CRM workflow or advanced enterprise control that is not confirmed in the plan. Build a short feature checklist and ask the vendor to show each required task.</p>
<h2 id="migration">Plan a safe Freshdesk migration</h2>
<p>Do not cancel Freshdesk until you have tested the destination. Freshdesk's official export guide says admins can export tickets, contacts, companies or granular account data in CSV or Excel. It also says an Admin or Supervisor role and the correct export permissions are required. The guide explains the ticket export flow, but it does not guarantee that every record or workflow will import into another product without mapping.</p>
<ol><li>Inventory the objects you need: tickets, comments, attachments, contacts, companies, custom fields, help articles, saved replies, automation rules and reports.</li><li>Export a representative sample with filters that match the data you plan to move.</li><li>Check record counts, timestamps, encodings and the fields that the destination supports.</li><li>Map custom fields and status values before importing.</li><li>Recreate routing, SLA policies and automation rules in the destination. Do not expect the export file to rebuild them.</li><li>Run a small test import and compare the result with the source records.</li><li>Get sign-off from the support owner before shutting off the old account.</li></ol>
<p>Use the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">official Freshdesk ticket export guide</a> and the destination vendor's migration documentation. If the migration involves compliance, retention or contractual obligations, confirm the process with your administrator before moving customer data.</p>
<h2 id="choose">How to choose the right alternative</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Start with the work model.</strong> Decide whether you need a ticketing system, shared inbox, CRM-based service or ecommerce support platform.</li><li><strong>List the must-have channels.</strong> Mark every channel as required or optional, then compare the exact plan that includes it.</li><li><strong>Calculate total cost.</strong> Include seats, usage charges, AI sessions or resolutions, extra inboxes, onboarding and taxes.</li><li><strong>Check data portability.</strong> Verify export permissions, supported formats, attachment handling and destination imports before signing.</li><li><strong>Test two real workflows.</strong> Use one routine request and one escalation to see how assignment, replies, reporting and history behave.</li><li><strong>Keep the migration reversible.</strong> Retain the source system until record checks and business-owner approval are complete.</li></ul>
<p>If you are still deciding whether Freshdesk itself is the right fit, read the <a href="/freshdesk/review/">Freshdesk review</a>, <a href="/freshdesk/pricing/">pricing guide</a> and <a href="/freshdesk/how-to-use/">setup guide</a>. For a direct comparison, see <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk</a>. The alternatives page is one part of the decision, not a substitute for checking your own workflow.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What is the closest alternative to Freshdesk?</h3><p>Zendesk is the closest direct comparison for a conventional ticket-based help desk. Help Scout is worth checking if you prefer a shared inbox, while Zoho Desk can suit a small team or an existing Zoho customer. The best fit depends on channels, plan limits and migration needs.</p>
<h3>Does Freshdesk have a free plan?</h3><p>The current US pricing page advertises a 14-day free trial and displays paid plans. Freshworks also says switching to its Free program is no longer self-service, so new buyers should confirm eligibility rather than assume a permanent free plan is available.</p>
<h3>Which Freshdesk alternative is best for a small team?</h3><p>Start by comparing Zoho Desk's free edition for three users and Help Scout's free plan for five users. Check the included inboxes, contacts, knowledge base and automation limits before choosing. Those limits are as important as the starting price.</p>
<h3>Which alternative is better for ecommerce?</h3><p>Gorgias is worth evaluating for ecommerce-specific support workflows and order context. Confirm the integrations and ticket-volume pricing that match your store before moving.</p>
<h3>Can I export tickets from Freshdesk?</h3><p>Freshdesk documents CSV and Excel exports for tickets and other records. The available fields and objects depend on permissions and the selected export process. Test the actual data you need and verify the destination import before switching.</p>
<h3>Should I switch only to save money?</h3><p>Not without checking the full cost. Compare agent seats, AI usage, extra channels, implementation, migration effort and the time needed to rebuild workflows. A lower headline price may not lower the total cost.</p>
